<!doctype html>
<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only

     The dev page. M2-H2 (#55) built it to prove frame, audio and input
     all cross the wasm boundary; M3-F2 (#84) added the one affordance
     M3's exit criterion needs — a way to put a player's own directory in
     front of the machine, because "verified locally on desktop and web"
     had no web half without it.

     Deliberately bare, still: this is scaffolding, not the M6 reference
     shell (no onboarding, no persistence, no touch, no styling worth the
     name). Nothing a player drops here is kept; a reload starts over.

     ES modules need a real origin, so open it over HTTP, not file://:
         cmake --build --preset wasm
         python3 scripts/serve-web.py

     A template, not a plain page: CMake substitutes
     AMBERFOLIO_SOURCE_URL into the source offer at the bottom (see
     hosts/web/CMakeLists.txt) — spelled without its at-signs here, or
     this comment would be substituted too. Nothing else is: the rule is
     @ONLY, so the ${...} in app.mjs/host.mjs is JS, not CMake.
-->
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Amber Folio — dev page</title>
    <style>
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 2rem;
        background: #14100c;
        color: #e8d9bd;
        font: 1rem/1.5 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
      }
      h1 {
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        font-weight: 600;
        margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
      }
      p.note {
        color: #b3a58c;
        margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
        max-width: 60ch;
      }
      #screen {
        display: block;
        image-rendering: pixelated;
        image-rendering: crisp-edges;
        width: 640px;
        height: 400px;
        background: #000;
        border: 1px solid #3a2f22;
      }
      .controls {
        margin: 1rem 0;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 1rem;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
      }
      fieldset {
        margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
        padding: 1rem;
        max-width: 640px;
        border: 1px solid #3a2f22;
      }
      legend {
        padding: 0 0.5rem;
        color: #e0a33c;
      }
      #drop {
        padding: 1.25rem;
        margin: 0 0 1rem;
        border: 1px dashed #5a4a30;
        color: #b3a58c;
        text-align: center;
      }
      #drop.dragging {
        border-color: #e0a33c;
        color: #e8d9bd;
      }
      label {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.5rem;
      }
      input[type='range'],
      input[type='checkbox'] {
        accent-color: #e0a33c;
      }
      input[type='range'] {
        width: 8rem;
      }
      select,
      input[type='text'],
      input[type='number'],
      input[type='file'] {
        font: inherit;
        background: #0a0805;
        color: #e8d9bd;
        border: 1px solid #5a4a30;
        padding: 0.35rem 0.5rem;
        max-width: 100%;
      }
      select:disabled {
        opacity: 0.6;
      }
      button {
        font: inherit;
        background: #2a2115;
        color: #e8d9bd;
        border: 1px solid #5a4a30;
        padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
        cursor: pointer;
      }
      button:disabled {
        cursor: default;
        opacity: 0.6;
      }
      #status {
        color: #b3a58c;
      }
      #health {
        color: #b3a58c;
        font-size: 0.875rem;
      }
      #console {
        margin: 1rem 0 0;
        padding: 0.75rem;
        max-width: 640px;
        max-height: 12rem;
        overflow-y: auto;
        white-space: pre-wrap;
        background: #0a0805;
        border: 1px solid #3a2f22;
      }
      footer {
        margin: 2rem 0 0;
        padding: 1rem 0 0;
        border-top: 1px solid #3a2f22;
        font-size: 0.875rem;
        color: #b3a58c;
        max-width: 640px;
      }
      footer a {
        color: #e0a33c;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Amber Folio — dev page</h1>
    <p class="note">
      This is scaffolding, not a game. Press <em>start</em> and it runs a
      built-in program that draws a pattern, plays a tone and echoes what
      you type — proof that video, sound and the keyboard all cross the
      wasm boundary. Or point it at a directory of your own below.
      Autoplay policy means the browser will not let a page start audio
      before you interact with it, so nothing runs until you do.
    </p>

    <div class="controls">
      <button id="start" type="button">start</button>
      <!-- The speed governor (#107), the same four presets the desktop
           host's --speed takes. A machine setting rather than a
           per-program one, which is why it is up here beside start and
           not inside the fieldset below: it applies to whichever of the
           two things this page can run. -->
      <label>
        speed
        <select id="speed">
          <option value="xt" selected>xt</option>
          <option value="turbo">turbo</option>
          <option value="at">at</option>
          <option value="386">386</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <!-- Volume and mute (#148), which are this host's and not the
           machine's: the gain is applied inside the speaker worklet, on
           the far side of the boundary, and nothing about it reaches the
           machine or is recorded. A run at 25% is the same run as one at
           100%. Two controls rather than one because mute is a latch —
           lifting it gives back the level that was there. -->
      <label>
        volume
        <input id="volume" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="100" />
      </label>
      <label>
        <input id="mute" type="checkbox" />
        mute
      </label>
      <span id="status">idle - click start</span>
    </div>

    <canvas id="screen" width="320" height="200"></canvas>

    <!-- What the run has got through, and what the audio path had to
         paper over on the way (#106's policies have a visible half, and
         this is it). Neither counter is machine state; both are
         host-pacing symptoms (platform.h). -->
    <div class="controls">
      <span id="health"></span>
    </div>

    <pre id="console"></pre>

    <!-- The M3-F2 (#84) half: a directory, a program, and the same stop
         report the desktop host prints. Nothing here is kept - the files
         live in the machine's own filesystem for as long as the page is
         open and no longer. -->
    <fieldset>
      <legend>run your own copy</legend>
      <div id="drop">drop a game directory here</div>
      <div class="controls">
        <label>
          directory
          <input id="directory" type="file" webkitdirectory directory multiple />
        </label>
      </div>
      <div class="controls">
        <label>
          program
          <select id="program" disabled></select>
        </label>
        <label>
          command tail
          <input id="tail" type="text" size="12" placeholder="(none)" />
        </label>
      </div>
      <div class="controls">
        <label>
          stop after
          <input id="steps" type="number" min="0" size="10" placeholder="(no limit)" />
          steps
        </label>
        <label>
          <input id="trace" type="checkbox" />
          keep a trace
        </label>
        <button id="boot" type="button" disabled>boot</button>
      </div>
      <!-- The M4-F4 (#98) half: every seam this build carries, listed once
           the program is loaded and identified, with a checkbox per seam
           — off by default, and disabled with the reason when the seam is
           not available for this program (PLAN.md §5). -->
      <div class="controls">
        <span id="edition"></span>
      </div>
      <div id="seams" class="controls"></div>
      <p class="note">
        Files are read in your browser and never leave it. A file whose
        name is not a legal DOS 8.3 name is refused by the emulator's own
        filesystem rules and listed as skipped — that refusal is the
        emulator being honest, not a fault.
      </p>
    </fieldset>

    <script type="module">
      // Imported dynamically, inside the try, rather than with a static
      // import at the top: a static one that fails takes the whole module
      // script with it, so the catch below never runs and the page sits
      // with the Start button doing nothing while saying why nowhere. That
      // is exactly the case worth reporting — a server that sends app.mjs
      // as text/plain, which browsers refuse to execute as a module, is
      // the usual way this page fails on a fresh machine.
      const status = document.getElementById('status');
      try {
        const { runDevPage } = await import('./app.mjs');
        runDevPage();
      } catch (error) {
        status.textContent = `failed to load: ${error}`;
        document.getElementById('start').disabled = true;
        console.error(error);
        throw error;
      }
    </script>

    <!-- Not decoration. AGPL-3.0-only §13: serving this program over a
         network obliges us to offer its users the Corresponding Source,
         and "the project is on GitHub somewhere" does not discharge
         that — the offer has to be reachable from the page, and it has
         to name the version being served. CI pins the URL to the commit
         it deployed; a local build points at the repository. -->
    <footer>
      Amber Folio is free software under AGPL-3.0-only.
      <a href="@AMBERFOLIO_SOURCE_URL@">Source code for this build</a>.
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
